// A temporal formula reading a CURRENT-REQUEST principal ENTITY ATTRIBUTE
// (`principal.dept`) — the capability added when `principal` / `resource`
// became first-class temporal roots (Cedar-consistent, mirroring the pure-Cedar
// `when { principal.dept }` and the provider-arg `principal.dept`).
//
// "Permit Read only if the SAME user logged in within the last hour AND the
// current principal's department is `eng`." The `formerly … Login{ input.user:
// context.input.user }` is the temporal correlation; `principal.dept == "eng"`
// reads the current request's principal attribute from the entity store. Before
// scope roots existed, a temporal formula had no way to read an entity
// attribute at all.
//
// The attribute read is a tp-independent conjunct (a fixed current-request
// value, like `context.input.x`), so — exactly as a `context.input.*`
// comparison would — it sits INSIDE the monitoring scope alongside the
// tp-dependent `formerly`, not as a bare top-level conjunct (which the
// tp-dependence check rejects as "monitors nothing"). Wrapping both conjuncts
// in a single parenthesized group under one `formerly`-bearing scope is the
// idiom corpus case 0735 uses for `context.input.amount == a`.
//
// Positive/negative controls across traces:
//   trace_1: prior Login by the same user, principal.dept == "eng" -> permit.
//   trace_2: identical, but principal.dept == "sales" -> the attribute clause
//            fails -> NOT permitted (isolates the attribute read).
//   trace_3: principal.dept == "eng" but NO prior Login -> the temporal
//            correlation fails -> NOT permitted (isolates the temporal half).

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h (
        Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
        && principal.dept == "eng"
    )
};
